(area "mcp" :when "the work needs a connected service, an MCP server or tool, or the user's own computer: finding, loading, signing in to or calling one")

(memory "internal/mcp/local" :when "the work happens on the user's own computer: their local files, git, a desktop app or a web browser to drive; or a local server failed to start"
  (format nil "User's own machine (their files, git, desktop app, browser on their computer) : servers run there through desktop app.
How-to is memory internal/mcp/local, loads itself when user asks; else (memory/recall \"internal/mcp/local\") .
User's own machine: predefined local servers, names start local- , e.g.
(load-mcp \"local-files\") , (load-mcp \"local-git\") . Run on user's machine
through desktop app. Only when user asks for their machine. Fails if desktop
app not open.
- (desktop-connected-p) : t if desktop app connected as of this step, else nil.
- Listed only while desktop app connected. Row ends :local , e.g.
(\"local-git\" \"…\" (\"git\" …) :unloaded :local) . No :local = runs on server.
- Row ends :also-local = toolkit server has own setup for user machine:
(load-mcp \"acme\" :local t) . Without :local t it runs on server. Browser
  automation on user's own browser is this: find browser server with
  search-mcps, load it :local t.
- List after :local or :also-local = flags server takes, e.g. (:quiet) :
(load-mcp \"acme\" :local t :quiet t) . Only listed flags; others error.
- Toolkit server with own command: (load-mcp \"acme\" :local t) runs that
  command, not container.
- Local load asks user as call ~a<name> , e.g. ~alocal-files ,
  unless config allows it: (permission/allow ~alocal-files) or
(permission/allow ~a*) .
- Load fails with message if desktop app not open or user denied: say so,
  don't retry." *mcp-local-gate* *mcp-local-gate* *mcp-local-gate* *mcp-local-gate*))

(memory "internal/mcp/oauth" :when "a connected service needs sign-in or authorization, a call just failed on expired or missing authorization, or the user wants to sign out"
  "OAuth remote: add :oauth t, optional :scopes; then login and mcp-authorize.
login/logout only for :oauth t servers:
- (login \"name\") gives :logged-in if authenticated. Else, like load needing
  login, asks user to authorize, ends turn; you are told when they answer.
- User authorized: run (await (load-mcp \"name\")) , carry on with original
  request; redirect captured for you. Only when they hand back bare code,
  run (mcp-authorize \"name\" \"<code>\") before that load. Never ask for code
  before that load failed.
- (logout \"name\") gives :logged-out. Unloads server if loaded AND deletes
  saved tokens, so next (load-mcp \"name\") or (login \"name\") re-authorizes
  from scratch. Use to sign out, or force fresh consent after widening
  :scopes, e.g. on 403 insufficient_scope: (logout \"name\") then
(login \"name\") again.
- Accept only predefined OAuth server name; unknown or non-OAuth errors.
  logout no-op-safe when server not loaded.")

(memory "internal/mcp/search" :when "the agent needs a capability or service nobody named and must find which MCP server or tool does the job"
  "Two searches, two jobs, don't confuse:
- (search-mcps \"query\") searches toolkit of predefined servers, loaded or not.
  Matches keywords, name, description, so search capability in own words:
  vision, spreadsheet, tickets, analytics. Don't guess product name.
  Misspelling still lands, prefix counts: read near miss before retyping. Also
(list-mcps) , and (list-toolkit) with every server's keywords.
- (search-tools \"query\") searches tools of ALL loaded servers by name and
  description, best-first, forgives misspelling. ONE query string, NO server
  argument.
(list-tools) lists tools of ALL loaded servers. (list-tools \"acme\" \"beta\")
lists only those servers. Unloaded name is error.
(search-tools \"acme\" \"widget\") is WRONG, an arity error. For one server: load
it, then (search-tools \"widget\") ; list with (list-tools \"acme\") .")

(memory "internal/mcp/timeout" :when "the agent is about to run a slow job through an MCP tool, such as a crawl, scrape, build, render, big export or long query, or a tool call just timed out"
  (format nil "Tool call waits ~d seconds, then fails \"acme/x timed out\".
Slow tool: add :mcp/timeout with seconds, above 0, max ~d, beside tool's own
keywords:
(acme/crawl_site :url \"https://...\" :mcp/timeout 600)
:mcp/timeout is yours, never sent to server. Set it up front when tool name or
args say slow. Call timed out? Retry once with larger :mcp/timeout, not same
call again. Still too slow? Split work into smaller calls." *mcp-tool-timeout-seconds* *mcp-max-tool-timeout-seconds*))

(memory "internal/mcp/result" :when "a connected service just returned a result and the agent must read values or report fields out of it"
  "Tool result is Lisp data: JSON object is alist with string keys, array is list.
Read with assoc, cdr, mapcar, nth. No parsing. JSON sent as text is parsed too,
so report names its keys. Only non-JSON string needs (json-parse s) .")

(memory "internal/mcp/example" :when "the agent is about to use an MCP tool end to end: find the server, load it, find the tool, call it"
  "Worked example, names made up:
(search-mcps \"widget\")
(await (load-mcp \"acme\"))
(search-tools \"widget\")
(acme/get_widget :id \"42\")
A loaded tool's (doc 'acme/list_widgets) prints full signature, every argument, which required.")

(memory "internal/mcp/load" :when "the agent is about to load a predefined or ad-hoc MCP server, several at once, or start a load now to use later; or a load just failed"
  "(load-mcp \"name\") loads predefined server. load-mcp async: returns promise at
once, NOT blocking; server connects in background; tools install when it
settles. Wrap SINGLE load-mcp call in await to use it in same step. Each
loaded tool becomes global named <server>/<tool>, called with keyword args,
blocking.
:command , :args , :env NOT supported: error. Only toolkit names and :url .
(await (load-mcp \"acme\")) blocks until ready, returns tools as
(name argument-count description) , same rows as (list-tools) , so no listing
after load.
  Do NOT call (load-mcp \"acme\") then (await (load-mcp \"acme\")) : TWO
  connections. Wrap directly, or bind one promise:
(setq p (load-mcp \"acme\")) then later (await p) .
- Ad-hoc remote server, awaited same way:
(load-mcp :name \"x\" :url \"https://...\" :headers '((\"Authorization\" . \"Bearer …\")))
- Several concurrently:
(await (promise-all-settled (list (load-mcp \"a\") (load-mcp \"b\")))) .")
